              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.15: Particles of soap help in cleaning"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.676,
            0.719,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The particulate nature of matter plays a crucial role in many everyday processes. For example, when we wash clothes stained with oil using soap, numerous soap particles surround the oil particles on the fabric. One end of the soap particle attaches to the oil, and the other mixes with water, thus helping lift the oil off and wash it away (Fig. 7.15)."
            }
          ]
        },
